{
  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a downtrend (e.g., falling from ~72 to ~61) and sentiment is neutral or slightly positive, the model systematically overestimates future prices by ignoring immediate mean reversion; apply a downward correction of 5-8% to the forecasted path.",
    "In volatile regimes with mixed sentiment (positive spikes followed by negative), the model exhibits significant lag in reacting to sudden price spikes; forecasts often fail to capture the full magnitude of upward momentum, requiring an upward bias adjustment when volatility increases.",
    "The model consistently underestimates short-term volatility and over-smooths price action during high-sentiment periods, leading to a 'flatline' forecast that misses sharp peaks; apply a variance correction factor to widen the confidence interval around the mean forecast.",
    "When sentiment history shows a recent negative spike (-1.0) followed by neutral values, the model tends to persistently overestimate recovery rates; reduce the slope of the forecasted curve by 20-30% to account for delayed market stabilization.",
    "In high-volatility environments (history range >15%), the model's linear extrapolation fails to capture mean-reverting behavior within the first 7 days; switch from a linear trend to a stochastic walk with higher drift uncertainty for the initial forecast horizon."
  ]
}
